.TH HOC 2
.SH NAME
hoc: compile, compileeqn, eval, evalarray, symbol, setvar, getvar, reset, modelcmd \- hoc expression language, as a library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "hoc.m";
hoc := load Hoc Hoc->PATH;

e := hoc->compile("av+h*(da*lap_av-av*bv*bv+feed*(1.0-av))");
if(e == nil)
	... # parse error already reported to stderr

s := hoc->symbol("av");
s.val = 0.5;
v := hoc->eval(e);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hoc
is
.IR hoc (1)'s
own expression grammar and evaluator, exported as an ordinary Inferno
library \(em a real, parsable equation language a caller can embed,
rather than a file/stdin-driven REPL. It backs
.B "equation hoc"
in
.IR pde (2)
and the hoc-driven models under
.IR appl/danby ,
but has no dependency on either: any caller wanting named-variable
expressions parsed once and evaluated many times can
.B include
it directly.
.PP
.B Compile
parses one hoc expression or assignment
(e.g.
.RB \*(lq dprey " = " birth*prey-predation*prey*predators \*(rq)
from a string and returns its tree, or
.B nil
on a parse error (already reported to stderr, in the same form
.IR hoc (1)
itself would use).  Every
.B Var
or
.B Assign
node inside the returned tree holds a
.B "ref Sym"
directly, so the SAME compiled tree can be
.B eval
ed again after rebinding a variable's value through
.BR symbol ()
\(em it does not need to be recompiled per call.  This is what makes
it usable inside a tight per-step loop: parse once per model load,
rebind and evaluate many times.
.PP
.B Compileeqn
is
.B compile
for a caller whose source is written in
.IR eqn (7)
notation (see
.IR eqn2hoc (2))
rather than hoc's own grammar directly \(em the eqn(7)\(->hoc(2)
translation happens HERE, inside the library, so a caller with
equations in eqn syntax (
.IR danby-aerobraking (1),
for one) never loads or even names
.IR eqn2hoc (2)
itself; it just hands
.B compileeqn
the eqn source, the same way it would hand
.B compile
hoc source directly. Returns
.RI ( nil ", an error string)
on either a translation error or a hoc compile error, since eqn source
has a translation stage
.B compile
alone does not.
.PP
.B Symbol
finds or creates a named variable and returns a live handle whose
.B val
can be read or written directly, with no further lookup \(em the
zero-lookup form for a hot path.
.B Setvar
and
.B getvar
are the occasional-use equivalents, by name.
.B Reset
starts a fresh symbol table (the same constants and builtins
.IR hoc (1)'s
own
.B init
installs); call it once before compiling a new, unrelated set of
expressions so an earlier caller's variable names cannot leak into a
new one.
.SS Array-context evaluation
.B Evalarray
walks the identical tree
.B compile
returned \(em the same
.BR Var / Assign
nodes, the same
.B Sym
handles \(em in a different context: once per CALL, over a whole array
of
.I n
elements, instead of once per ELEMENT.  Any
.B Sym
referenced by the tree whose
.B vals
field
.RB ( "array of real" ,
zero elsewhere in this interface) is non-nil contributes its array
elementwise, in lockstep across all such symbols; any
.B Sym
whose
.B vals
is
.B nil
contributes its scalar
.B val
instead, broadcast across every element.  A caller with a whole PDE
field's worth of values per named variable binds each field's
.B Sym.vals
once per step, then calls
.B evalarray
once per field per substep \(em the dispatch cost of walking the tree
is paid once per node, not once per node per cell, which is what makes
this practical where calling
.B eval
in a per-cell loop is not (roughly two orders of magnitude slower at a
realistic Gray-Scott-shaped 128x128/four-substep/two-field workload;
see
.IR doc/danby-tks-plan.md
in the source tree for the measurement).
.PP
Real, stated limits, not oversights:
.BR Assign / PreIncDec / PostIncDec / Call / ReadE
nodes all have side effects or read from the input stream, neither of
which means anything applied to a whole array at once \(em unsupported,
reported once (not once per element) and a zero result.  Division and
.B %
by zero inside a binary node are silent IEEE
.B Inf
/
.B NaN
here, unlike
.BR eval 's
own reported error \(em a whole-grid walk expects to cross a zero
divisor routinely (a boundary cell mid-transient, say), and one
message per bad cell would be useless noise.  A built-in function
call
.RB ( sin ", " sqrt ", " log ", " ... )
still goes through the same domain check
.B eval
uses, so a genuine domain error (the square root of a negative number,
say) is still reported, once per bad element.
.I Hoc
has no clamp, min, or max built in at all \(em a caller wanting bounded
output must express that itself, or accept unbounded output.
.SS "Model scripts"
.B Param
/
.B maxtime
/
.B state
/
.B derivative
/
.B eq
/
.B observable
all mean exactly one thing: bind a name to a compiled expression (or
a plain number) in this instance's own symbol table \(em the same
vocabulary
.B compile
/
.B symbol
already have, just organized into a small named-equation SYSTEM
(parameters, state variables with their own derivatives, named
intermediate quantities, and diagnostic observables) instead of one
expression at a time.
.B Modelcmd
dispatches ONE already-tokenized line \(em the caller's own generic
tokenizer has already split it into words (
.IR string (2)'s
own
.BR fields () ,
say) \(em splitting a string is not this library's concern,
recognizing what the words MEAN is.  This is the same shape
.IR tk (2)'s
own
.B tk->cmd()
and
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.B Plotter.cmd()
already are: one line in, effect immediate (compiling its own
.IR eqn (7)
expression and growing the running tables right there, via
.BR compileeqn ,
never staged for a later pass), claimed-or-not-plus-error out.
.B Modelcmd
returns
.RI ( 0 ", " nil )
if the line's first word is none of the six above \(em NOT an error,
just "not my vocabulary": the caller should try another parser (its
own plain data, or another library's own command language \(em
exactly how a shell dispatches its builtins vs an external command,
never by rejecting anything a builtin doesn't recognize).  It returns
.RI ( 1 ", " nil )
if handled, or
.RI ( 1 ", an error string)
if the line WAS this vocabulary but malformed or failed to compile.  A
.B derivative
line's matching
.B state
line must already have run \(em the same "define before use" order an
ordinary script already requires;
.B modelcmd
names this specifically if it hasn't.
.PP
.B Modelreset
clears every model-script table built so far and starts a fresh
symbol table (it calls
.B reset
internally) \(em call it once before running a new, unrelated script.
.BR Modelparams ", " modelmaxtime ", " modelstatenames ", and " modelobslabels
are read-back accessors for what the script has built;
.B modelparams
hands back the SAME live arrays a caller should write through directly
to change a running value (a Tk scale widget's own callback, say), not
a snapshot copy.
.B Modelinitialstate
evaluates every
.B state
line's own initial-value expression, after binding every param's
current value.
.B Modelrhs
matches
.IR numerics (2)'s
own
.B Rhs
function type exactly, so a caller integrating this model can pass
.B hoc->modelrhs
directly to
.BR numerics->rk4 ()
et al.\& with no wrapper of its own; it binds params and the given
state values, evaluates every
.B eq
line in declaration order, then every
.B derivative
line.
.B Modelobservables
does the same
.B eq
pass, then evaluates every
.B observable
line, returning one real per
.B observable
command.
.B Modelcheck
\(em call once after a whole script has run \(em reports whether
every
.B state
command got its own matching
.BR derivative ,
.B nil
if so; this can only be known once the whole script has run, so it is
not part of
.B modelcmd
itself, the same reason a caller already does its own separate
cross-checks (a title got set, param/body counts match, ...) after the
script finishes rather than inside any one line's own effect.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/hoc.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/hoc.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hoc (1),
.IR hoctest (1),
.IR pde (2),
.IR pdehoctest (1),
.IR eqn2hoc (2),
.IR danby-aerobraking (1),
.IR eqnorbitaltest (1)
.SH BUGS
.B Evalarray
always returns a freshly allocated array, even when the whole
expression is a single bare variable reference \(em otherwise that one
case would hand back the CALLER's own bound array
.RI ( Sym.vals )
instead of a new result, aliasing it to whatever the caller does with
the return value next.  Every other expression shape already allocates
its own result on the way up the tree; this is the one path that would
not have, so it is special-cased rather than left as a trap for a
rhs that happens to be just a field's own name.
.PP
Two
.B Hoc
instances (two
.B load
calls) never share a symbol table, by design \(em a compiled tree's
.B Sym
handles are tied to the specific instance that compiled them.  A
caller running two independent equations at once (two
.IR pde (2)
problems, say) must
.B load
a separate instance per equation; sharing one would let their variable
names silently collide. The same rule covers every model-script table
in the section above \(em two instances never share a
.BR param / state / eq / observable
table either.
